          "ruleId": "CWE-200: Exposure of Sensitive Information to an Unauthorized Actor",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Sensitive local system paths and internal addresses leaked in audit logs  [CVSS 5.3: CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N]"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "piolium/audit-state.json"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 18
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "severity": "medium",
            "security-severity": "5.3",
            "cvssRating": "Medium",
            "category": "CWE-200: Exposure of Sensitive Information to an Unauthorized Actor",
            "cvssVector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N",
            "cwe": "CWE-200 - Exposure of Sensitive Information to an Unauthorized Actor",
            "cweId": "CWE-200",
            "cweName": "Exposure of Sensitive Information to an Unauthorized Actor",
            "cvssScore": 5.3,
            "offensivePriority": "P3",
            "offensivePriorityLabel": "Internal Network / Privileged Position",
            "offensivePriorityReason": "Internal Network / Privileged Position | *internal-network position required*",
            "confidence": 0.95,
            "description": "#### Description\nThe `error` and `last_error` fields in `audit-state.json` contain unmasked absolute Windows filesystem paths (e.g., `C:\\Users\\jsoehner\\AppData\\...`) and internal service URLs (`http://127.0.0.1:8080`). This data is likely written to the state file by error handlers during runtime without sanitization of the underlying exception messages.\n\n#### Impact\nAn attacker accessing the audit state can discover absolute filesystem paths on the host machine and internal network addresses. This information facilitates targeted attacks, such as identifying software versions for exploitation or preparing path traversal attacks.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAn attacker querying the Supervisor Agent's REST API (an untrusted entry point) retrieves the audit state JSON, revealing the host's local username and the exact location of sensitive npm modules on the developer's or server's filesystem.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Attacker can access the `audit-state.json` file via the Supervisor Agent REST API or repository access\n\n```\n\"error\": \"Failed after 5 retries: No API key found for llama-server=http://127.0.0.1:8080.\\n\\nUse /login to log into a provider via OAuth or API key. See:\\n  C:\\\\Users\\\\jsoehner\\\\AppData\\\\Roaming\\\\npm\\\\node_modules\\\\@earendil-works\\\\pi-coding-agent\\\\docs\\\\providers.md...\"\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nImplement a sanitization layer in the error handling logic to strip absolute filesystem paths, local user directories, and internal IP addresses from error messages before they are persisted to audit logs or state files.\n\n**Exploitability:** (not ranked by chaining pass)\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 10/10) \u2014 The `audit-state.json` file contains unmasked absolute filesystem paths containing the local username and internal service URLs, which constitutes an information leak.\n\nThe scanner has correctly identified that `piolium/audit-state.json` contains sensitive information, specifically absolute Windows filesystem paths (revealing the local username `jsoehner`) and internal service URLs (`http://127.0.0.1:8080`).\n\nWhile the \"exploit scenario\" assumes an attacker can access this file via a REST API, the primary issue is indeed the presence of unmasked, sensitive data in a state file that is intended to be used for audit logging and potentially exposed through other interfaces. The content itself is verified by the `Read` operation on the file.\n\nThe impact of leaking local usernames and internal network addresses is a real information leak (PII/system metadata), as it aids an attacker in reconnaissance and lateral movement within a compromised environment."
          },
